WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
In March 2023, millions of fish died in

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.000
the Darling-Baaka River at Menindee.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.720
The New South Wales Chief Scientist's review found

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.200
that low dissolved oxygen was the most likely

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.720
cause, and fisheries reports noted deaths around first

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:15.480
light.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.200
A reading at noon could have looked fine.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.600
So what do dissolved oxygen and BOD tell

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.920
us, and how do chemists measure them?

00:00:25.134 --> 00:00:29.053
Dissolved oxygen, or DO, is the oxygen gas

00:00:29.094 --> 00:00:32.013
dissolved in the water, measured in milligrams per

00:00:32.094 --> 00:00:32.853
litre.

00:00:33.214 --> 00:00:35.613
Fish and other water animals breathe it through

00:00:35.694 --> 00:00:36.653
their gills.

00:00:36.974 --> 00:00:40.493
Air-saturated fresh water at twenty degrees contains about

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:43.773
nine milligrams per litre, and warmer water holds

00:00:43.854 --> 00:00:44.573
less.

00:00:44.894 --> 00:00:47.213
When DO falls to just a few milligrams

00:00:47.294 --> 00:00:49.533
per litre, many fish struggle.

00:00:50.734 --> 00:00:52.893
But DO changes through the day.

00:00:53.374 --> 00:00:55.053
In a lake full of algae it can

00:00:55.094 --> 00:00:57.293
be high in the afternoon and lowest at

00:00:57.374 --> 00:01:00.213
dawn, so one reading is a snapshot.

00:01:00.494 --> 00:01:02.733
A DO meter puts a probe straight into

00:01:02.774 --> 00:01:05.053
the water, gives a reading in seconds, and

00:01:05.134 --> 00:01:06.333
can log all night.

00:01:06.894 --> 00:01:09.373
Meters are calibrated and checked against a chemical

00:01:09.454 --> 00:01:11.613
method, the Winkler titration.

00:01:12.834 --> 00:01:14.913
The Winkler method starts at the river.

00:01:15.394 --> 00:01:17.953
The bottle is filled to overflowing, with no

00:01:18.034 --> 00:01:19.553
air bubbles, and stoppered.

00:01:20.034 --> 00:01:23.073
That matters, because air contains oxygen, and a

00:01:23.154 --> 00:01:24.753
single bubble would add some.

00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:27.553
Then the oxygen is fixed straight away, so

00:01:27.634 --> 00:01:29.473
it can't escape or change.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:31.420
Step one.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.740
Add manganese two sulfate, then an alkaline iodide

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:36.380
solution.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.900
A pale manganese two hydroxide forms, and the

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.860
dissolved oxygen oxidises it straight away to a

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:43.820
brown solid.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.780
Every oxygen molecule in the bottle is now

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.340
locked up in that brown solid, and nothing

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:50.940
can get in or out.

00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:52.966
Step two.

00:01:53.447 --> 00:01:54.726
Add sulfuric acid.

00:01:55.207 --> 00:01:57.926
The brown solid dissolves, and its manganese is

00:01:58.007 --> 00:02:00.806
now strong enough to oxidise iodide ions to

00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:01.526
iodine.

00:02:02.007 --> 00:02:03.846
The solution turns yellow-brown.

00:02:04.407 --> 00:02:06.646
The more oxygen there was, the more brown

00:02:06.727 --> 00:02:09.366
solid formed, and the more iodine you get.

00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:11.366
Step three.

00:02:11.847 --> 00:02:14.246
A measured portion goes into a flask, and

00:02:14.327 --> 00:02:17.686
sodium thiosulfate from a burette turns the iodine

00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:19.206
back into iodide.

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:22.246
As the yellow fades to pale straw, add

00:02:22.287 --> 00:02:23.286
a little starch.

00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:26.806
It turns blue-black with iodine, and the endpoint

00:02:26.887 --> 00:02:28.806
is the moment the blue disappears.

00:02:29.447 --> 00:02:32.766
So thiosulfate never meets the oxygen itself.

00:02:32.967 --> 00:02:35.926
It measures the iodine, and the iodine stands

00:02:36.007 --> 00:02:37.126
in for the oxygen.

00:02:38.334 --> 00:02:41.093
So how much thiosulfate per oxygen?

00:02:41.374 --> 00:02:42.773
Follow the equations.

00:02:43.054 --> 00:02:45.933
One oxygen molecule fixes two manganese.

00:02:46.414 --> 00:02:49.693
Each manganese frees one iodine, so that's two

00:02:49.774 --> 00:02:50.333
iodine.

00:02:50.814 --> 00:02:54.653
And each iodine takes two thiosulfates, so four

00:02:54.734 --> 00:02:56.013
thiosulfates in all.

00:02:56.494 --> 00:02:59.053
One oxygen, four thiosulfates.

00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:01.346
Let's work one.

00:03:01.627 --> 00:03:04.586
A hundred millilitre sample needs eight point zero

00:03:04.747 --> 00:03:07.866
zero millilitres of point zero one zero zero

00:03:08.107 --> 00:03:09.466
molar thiosulfate.

00:03:10.027 --> 00:03:13.266
Find the dissolved oxygen in milligrams per litre.

00:03:13.627 --> 00:03:15.706
Start with the solution you know both numbers

00:03:15.787 --> 00:03:16.106
for.

00:03:16.587 --> 00:03:20.026
Moles equals c times V: point zero one

00:03:20.107 --> 00:03:23.146
zero zero times point zero zero eight zero

00:03:23.227 --> 00:03:26.426
zero litres is eight point zero zero times

00:03:26.507 --> 00:03:29.226
ten to the minus five moles of thiosulfate.

00:03:30.434 --> 00:03:31.953
Now use the ratio.

00:03:32.194 --> 00:03:35.633
Divide by four: two point zero zero times

00:03:35.714 --> 00:03:38.033
ten to the minus five moles of oxygen.

00:03:38.274 --> 00:03:41.473
Times thirty two gives six point four zero

00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:44.273
times ten to the minus four grams, which

00:03:44.354 --> 00:03:47.633
is point six four zero milligrams, all in

00:03:47.714 --> 00:03:49.473
that hundred millilitre sample.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.660
We want milligrams per litre, so divide by

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.380
the sample's volume in litres, not by the

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:56.940
titre.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.380
Point six four zero over point one zero

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.660
zero zero is six point four zero milligrams

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:04.540
per litre.

00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:07.766
DO tells you the oxygen now.

00:04:08.247 --> 00:04:12.486
Biochemical oxygen demand, or BOD, measures how much

00:04:12.647 --> 00:04:15.846
oxygen microbes use in five days, under standard

00:04:15.927 --> 00:04:16.886
conditions.

00:04:17.207 --> 00:04:19.366
Fill two bottles from the same sample.

00:04:19.847 --> 00:04:22.286
Measure the DO in one straight away.

00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:24.326
Seal the other and keep it dark at

00:04:24.407 --> 00:04:25.286
twenty degrees.

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:28.246
Sealed, so no new oxygen gets in.

00:04:28.727 --> 00:04:31.206
Dark, so algae can't make oxygen.

00:04:31.687 --> 00:04:34.846
Twenty degrees, so every lab's results compare.

00:04:35.127 --> 00:04:36.566
Then measure it again.

00:04:36.807 --> 00:04:39.366
The oxygen that's gone was used by microbes

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:41.126
breaking down organic matter.

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:43.846
So a high BOD means lots of oxygen

00:04:44.007 --> 00:04:46.646
used, not lots of oxygen present.

00:04:47.867 --> 00:04:51.786
So BOD five is the starting DO minus

00:04:51.867 --> 00:04:53.786
the DO after five days.

00:04:54.347 --> 00:04:56.586
A sample starts at eight point four, and

00:04:56.667 --> 00:04:58.826
after five days it's down to four point

00:04:58.907 --> 00:04:59.706
nine.

00:04:59.787 --> 00:05:02.506
That's a BOD of three point five milligrams

00:05:02.587 --> 00:05:03.466
per litre.

00:05:03.787 --> 00:05:06.346
Microbes in each litre used three point five

00:05:06.507 --> 00:05:09.786
milligrams of oxygen in five days, moderate on

00:05:09.867 --> 00:05:11.226
this card's teaching guide.

00:05:12.434 --> 00:05:13.873
Read the two together.

00:05:14.194 --> 00:05:17.473
Low DO with a high BOD means the

00:05:17.554 --> 00:05:20.033
water is short of oxygen and still losing

00:05:20.114 --> 00:05:22.033
it, which is the pattern before a fish

00:05:22.114 --> 00:05:22.433
kill.

00:05:23.074 --> 00:05:26.033
But BOD only sees what microbes can break

00:05:26.114 --> 00:05:26.513
down.

00:05:27.074 --> 00:05:32.433
BOD doesn't identify metals, pesticides or disease-causing microbes,

00:05:32.474 --> 00:05:35.233
and a poison can even stop the microbes,

00:05:35.314 --> 00:05:37.153
so the BOD reads low.

00:05:37.714 --> 00:05:40.593
A low BOD doesn't prove water is safe.

00:05:41.074 --> 00:05:43.473
And it takes five days, so for tonight,

00:05:43.554 --> 00:05:44.833
you still need the probe.

00:05:46.034 --> 00:05:47.233
Two lines to keep.

00:05:47.714 --> 00:05:49.633
DO is oxygen now.

00:05:50.114 --> 00:05:52.673
BOD is oxygen used later.

00:05:53.154 --> 00:05:56.433
And for the Winkler calculation, one oxygen, four

00:05:56.514 --> 00:05:57.713
thiosulfates.

00:05:58.934 --> 00:05:59.573
Your turn.

00:06:00.054 --> 00:06:02.933
A fifty millilitre river sample is analysed by

00:06:02.974 --> 00:06:05.653
the Winkler method, and the iodine needs ten

00:06:05.814 --> 00:06:08.853
point four zero millilitres of point zero zero

00:06:09.054 --> 00:06:11.973
five zero zero molar thiosulfate.

00:06:12.214 --> 00:06:15.733
Calculate the dissolved oxygen in milligrams per litre.

00:06:15.814 --> 00:06:17.093
Pause here to answer.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:31.020
Moles of thiosulfate, c times V, is five

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:33.340
point two zero times ten to the minus

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:34.140
five.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:37.020
Divide by four for oxygen, then times thirty

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:40.220
two: point four one six milligrams in fifty

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.340
millilitres.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.300
That's eight point three two milligrams per litre.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:47.340
Using one to one gives thirty three, about

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.260
four times the right answer.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:50.460
Recheck the ratio.

00:06:51.667 --> 00:06:52.306
Last one.

00:06:52.947 --> 00:06:55.586
Creek water is sampled above and below a

00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:59.586
food-processing plant's outlet, and each sample's oxygen is

00:06:59.667 --> 00:07:02.586
measured before and after five days.

00:07:02.947 --> 00:07:06.786
Calculate the five-day oxygen decrease for each sample,

00:07:06.947 --> 00:07:09.666
then explain what the result below the outlet

00:07:09.947 --> 00:07:12.226
suggests about the creek downstream.

00:07:12.547 --> 00:07:13.906
Pause here to answer.

00:07:24.334 --> 00:07:27.533
P's oxygen fell by point nine, and Q's

00:07:27.614 --> 00:07:30.173
by five point eight milligrams per litre.

00:07:30.494 --> 00:07:33.693
Q carries much more organic waste, so microbes

00:07:33.774 --> 00:07:35.853
will keep using oxygen as the water flows

00:07:35.934 --> 00:07:39.293
on, and DO downstream is likely to fall,

00:07:39.574 --> 00:07:41.133
especially on warm nights.

00:07:41.454 --> 00:07:44.173
Calling Q's decrease one point eight is the

00:07:44.254 --> 00:07:44.733
trap.

00:07:45.134 --> 00:07:48.413
That's the oxygen left, not the oxygen used.

00:07:49.634 --> 00:07:52.193
So DO is the oxygen in the water

00:07:52.274 --> 00:07:55.873
right now, and BOD is the oxygen used

00:07:56.034 --> 00:07:57.473
during the five-day test.

00:07:58.114 --> 00:08:00.753
Read them together, and remember what neither can

00:08:00.834 --> 00:08:01.233
see.

00:08:02.114 --> 00:08:05.553
Next, Module 8 turns to organic compounds, and

00:08:05.634 --> 00:08:07.953
the simple tests that tell their functional groups

00:08:08.034 --> 00:08:08.673
apart.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.340
Here are the key notes.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:13.500
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:14.380
to copy them.
